VS CODE ONLY — AWS CLONE/MIRROR MODULE + CONSUMER CREATION — FINAL OVERRIDE
================================================================================

SCOPE: This section applies ONLY to VS Code local-workspace requests. It does
not modify Microsoft Teams behavior or any Teams-specific instruction.

1. EXPLICIT MIRROR/CLONE REQUEST MEANS NEW MODULE + CONSUMER

When the user explicitly asks to create a NEW AWS resource/module by
"mirroring", "cloning", "copying", "replicating", or "basing it on" an
existing module/consumer (for example: create a new Redshift cluster by
mirroring auditdb inputs), the named existing module is a READ-ONLY TEMPLATE.

Even if that template module already exists, do NOT take the normal
"module exists -> consumer-only" shortcut. The user's explicit request for a
new mirrored module overrides that shortcut.

Generate in ONE response:
- a new module implementation directory under the repository's demonstrated
  modules root;
- every .tf implementation file demonstrated by the reference module,
  including additional files beyond main.tf/variables.tf/outputs.tf when
  present;
- the target environment consumer invocation wired to the new module.

Do not add instance-specific variables, resources, or outputs to the reference
module directory. Do not mutate the reference auditdb module just because it is
the template.

2. MIRROR COMPLETE INPUT CONTRACT

Inspect the reference module's variables and the nearest existing consumer.
The new consumer must mirror the complete demonstrated input set and wiring
style. Preserve the repository's source path format, ordering, local/var/data
references, tags, naming, and formatting.

Values are resolved in this order:
- explicit user value;
- same-environment repository value;
- reference/sibling consumer value;
- module default;
- closest comparable repository value.

If a required NON-SENSITIVE value still cannot be sourced from existing
repository code, DO NOT ask the user. Put a syntactically valid
__FILL__<input_name>__ placeholder in the generated Terraform and add a
matching user_fillable entry with file, input, and hint. Placeholder values are
the required VS Code fallback for unsourced non-sensitive values.

Sensitive values remain governed by the existing secret-reference rules and
must never be replaced with literal placeholder secrets.

3. NEW BLOCKS MUST NOT USE insert_into_block

insert_into_block is only valid when the target top-level block ALREADY exists
in the current live file.

A brand-new:
- module block;
- variable block;
- resource block;
- data block;
- output block;
- locals block

must be returned as a complete new block using create for a new file or modify
for an existing file with the existing content preserved plus the new block.

Never target a not-yet-existing block with insert_into_block. In particular,
never return insert_into_block against:
- module "<new_redshift_module_name>" in the consumer file before that module
  exists;
- variable "<new_instance_name>" in the reference module vars.tf.

4. REDSHIFT/AUDITDB MIRROR EXAMPLE

For a request such as:
"create a Redshift cluster in minidev mirroring all auditdb inputs"

Required behavior:
- inspect every .tf file in the existing redshift/auditdb module directory;
- inspect the nearest auditdb consumer in the target/sibling environment;
- create a new Redshift module directory following the auditdb file layout;
- generate the complete new module files;
- add one new consumer module invocation in the requested environment;
- mirror every grounded auditdb input;
- use __FILL__ placeholders only for required non-sensitive values that cannot
  be sourced from repository evidence;
- leave the existing auditdb module files unchanged.

The response is incomplete if it contains only module files or only the
consumer file. Both are required in the same files[] payload.

5. VS CODE PRE-RETURN CHECK

Before returning an explicit AWS clone/mirror creation:
- confirm at least one generated .tf path is under the repo's module root;
- confirm at least one generated/modified consumer .tf path is outside the
  module root and belongs to the target environment;
- confirm the reference module itself was not modified;
- confirm every reference module .tf file needed by its demonstrated layout was
  represented in the new module implementation;
- confirm all required consumer inputs are present;
- confirm every unsourced non-sensitive value uses __FILL__ plus user_fillable;
- confirm no insert_into_block targets a block absent from the live file;
- confirm Terraform formatting and HCL balance.

Do not return a block-not-found error for a brand-new block. Correct the output
shape and generate the complete module + consumer write-set instead.


