14. DIAGNOSTIC LOGGING (maintainers' note, not agent-facing)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The backend now emits one structured log line per pipeline stage — request
received, each validator's pass/fail with the exact error, which repair
strategy was chosen (tail-completion vs. full regeneration), each repair
call to the agent and what came back, and the final commit success or
exhausted-attempts failure. Every line is tagged `[TerrabotDiag]` and is a
single `event=... key=value ...` line, so the whole "backend asks agent /
agent responds / check fails / agent fixes it" story is readable directly
from the Function App Log Stream without needing to read source code. This
does not change any agent-facing behavior — it exists purely so failures can
be diagnosed (and fixes like Section 13 built) from what actually happened
in production, like this one was.

FOLLOW-UP FIX — the logs weren't showing up at all: the first version of
`_teams_diag_log` logged most events at INFO severity, and the logger's
effective level was never explicitly raised anywhere in the service. Under
the common default (root logger effectively at WARNING, which is typical for
an Azure Functions Python worker unless `host.json`/`logging.basicConfig`
says otherwise), every INFO-level call was silently dropped before it ever
reached the log stream — so the code was deployed and running (confirmed:
the exhaustion message the user saw used the new, updated error wording),
but its own diagnostics were invisible. Fixed by making `_teams_diag_log`
always emit at WARNING severity regardless of its `level` argument (kept
only as a readability tag inside the message text), plus a redundant stdout
`print()` as a second, independent channel, plus explicitly setting the
logger's own level. Verified against a simulated "root logger at WARNING,
no basicConfig" environment — both info- and warning-tagged diag calls now
surface. If diagnostics are STILL not visible after this fix, the next
thing to check is the Function App's own logLevel filtering in
`host.json` (the `logging.logLevel` section) — that operates a layer above
anything this service's own Python logging calls can control.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15. ASK-BEFORE-GENERATING, PUSH-THE-WHOLE-SET-TOGETHER (AGENT-SIDE, HARD)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A real production failure combined two mistakes in one response for "create a
storage account 'storageaccount1' in sbx-infra":

1. The agent's own analysis said it would add a dedicated object to
   hub.tfvars now, and asked the user to confirm ZRS vs GRS before it would
   "append the matching module block in storage_accounts.tf and declare the
   variable in variables.tf" as a LATER step. But the branch it actually
   pushed contained ONLY storage_accounts.tf — hub.tfvars was never touched,
   variables.tf was never touched — and the module block it pushed already
   referenced `var.storage_account_storageaccount1.*`, a variable that was
   declared nowhere and assigned nowhere. The response described a plan
   across multiple future turns instead of doing the whole thing at once,
   and additionally the "now" part of that plan wasn't even true.
2. Separately, a genuinely blocking Bucket C question (ZRS-style vs
   GRS-style replication — the repo demonstrates both, the user's prompt
   named neither) should have been asked BEFORE any file was generated, not
   narrated mid-generation as a thing to confirm after some files were
   already pushed.

FIX — two hard rules:

A. RESOLVE EVERY TRULY BLOCKING QUESTION BEFORE GENERATING ANYTHING.

For ordinary Azure object-backed resource creation with an existing
resource-family pattern, repository-derived decisions are NOT blocking
questions and MUST be resolved automatically from live repository evidence.

This includes:

- selecting the nearest existing sibling instance;
- selecting the repository pattern (including ZRS/GRS or equivalent sibling variant)
  when the user did not explicitly specify one;
- deriving the dedicated object-root variable name;
- appending the required variable declaration to the existing variables.tf;
- selecting the correct target environment values file (hub.tfvars,
  tier.tfvars, common.tfvars, or the repository's demonstrated equivalent);
- cloning the nearest sibling's object schema;
- cloning the nearest sibling's grounded non-sensitive values and applying
  only the user's explicit overrides.

These are repository decisions, not user decisions.

The only permitted pre-generation question for this Azure object-backed
creation workflow is an exact-name collision where the requested resource
already exists and the user must choose between modifying the existing
resource or creating one under a different name.

Editing the existing variables.tf file to append the dedicated object-root
variable declaration is automatically authorized as part of this workflow
and MUST NEVER require user confirmation.

For genuinely blocking Bucket C situations outside this workflow, return
empty files[] and ask exactly one blocking question before generating files.

B. ONCE UNBLOCKED, PUSH THE COMPLETE WRITE-SET IN ONE RESPONSE. For any
   creation that needs a definition-file block, a variable declaration, and
   a values-file assignment (the normal three-file Azure object-backed
   shape from Section 2 step 5), all three go in the SAME `files[]` array of
   the SAME response — never split across turns, never "I'll do the rest
   after you confirm X" when X has already been confirmed or was never
   actually blocking. `analysis`/`summary` must only describe files that are
   actually present in `files[]` for THIS response — never narrate a file as
   done, or as coming next, that isn't in this exact payload.

C. VALUES ALWAYS GO IN THE TARGET ENVIRONMENT'S OWN VALUES FILE, FOLLOWING
   THAT FOLDER'S OWN CONVENTION FOR THIS RESOURCE FAMILY. Don't guess a
