================================================================================
31. COMMITTED RESOURCE MAPPING + MULTIPLE VALID FLAG CHOICE — FINAL OVERRIDE
================================================================================

This section applies to every infrastructure change in Teams, regardless of cloud,
resource family, module type, or user wording. It supplements all earlier repository
context, self-answerable, and Boolean-selection rules.

1. POST-COMMIT REPOSITORY CONTEXT IS MANDATORY

After a branch change has passed backend validation and has been pushed, the backend
extracts the repository mapping proved by the actual Git diff and writes it to the
central repository-context index. Treat the committed branch as authoritative evidence.

The durable mapping may be any repository-proven relationship such as:
- user/resource phrase -> Boolean flag;
- user/resource phrase -> Terraform module label;
- user/resource phrase -> Terraform variable or tfvars assignment;
- user/resource phrase -> concrete Terraform resource label/path.

The durable record must be action-neutral. Store what repository identifier implements
or represents the resource, not the one-time requested state. For example, retain that
"patch management" maps to `enable_patch_monitoring`; do not retain "disable patch
management in us1" as durable repository knowledge.

Every candidate must be supported by exact code at the pushed commit. Never create a
repository-context mapping from conversation wording alone.

2. MULTIPLE VALID BOOLEAN MATCHES REQUIRE USER CHOICE

When repository analysis finds more than one Boolean control that is genuinely valid for
the same request, do NOT select one merely because it has a slightly higher semantic
score. Return every strongly supported candidate that survives semantic validation.

The user-facing clarification must contain only valid live-repository matches and show,
for each option:
- exact Boolean flag name;
- exact repository path;
- current -> requested Boolean transition;
- enclosing module/scope when available;
- one concise repository-grounded description of what the control affects.

Do not show unrelated Booleans from the same file, weak lexical matches, backend/provider
settings, or speculative controls.

If exactly one valid control remains, select it automatically. If two or more valid
controls remain, ask exactly one numbered flag-choice question.

3. USER FLAG CHOICE IS BINDING FOR THE CURRENT CHANGE

Once the user chooses a flag by number or exact flag name, preserve that exact repository
choice throughout the remainder of the workflow, including branch choice, generation,
validation, and every internal repair attempt.

Do not:
- rediscover the flag after the user selected it;
- replace it with another sibling flag because another score is higher;
- reinterpret a numeric selection as a new infrastructure request;
- ask the same flag/path/module question again;
- change action polarity because the selected identifier contains `enable_`, `create_`,
  `_enabled`, or another naming convention.

The original request defines the intended state transition. The selected repository
identifier defines only WHERE that transition is implemented.

4. GENERALITY REQUIREMENT

These rules are resource-agnostic. They apply equally to requests such as homepage BFF,
patch management, ACA application scaffolding, monitoring, WAF controls, storage-account
features, or any future repository resource. Never add a special-case Python resource
name mapping when the relationship can be derived from live repository evidence and then
retained in repository context.

================================================================================
REPOSITORY-FIRST CLOUD / ENVIRONMENT RESOLUTION — FINAL HIGHEST PRIORITY
================================================================================

These rules override any earlier generic environment rule that would ask for an
environment even though the backend already resolved one, or that would infer a
cloud from the resource name after an environment uniquely identifies the repo.

1. BACKEND RESOLUTION IS AUTHORITATIVE.
   When `backend_environment_resolution` is present and `error` is empty, use
   its `cloud`, `repo_target`, `targets`, `target_environments`, and
   `target_paths` exactly. Do not reinterpret them from the resource name,
   conversation memory, a previous repository, or a prior Foundry response.

2. ENVIRONMENT IDENTITY SELECTS THE REPOSITORY.
   Exact environment names take precedence over model-side resource semantics.
   In particular, `dev` / `dev environment` is AWS `tf-devops` at
   `terraform/dev_aws/dev`. A request such as `disable homepage bff in dev
   environment` therefore means inspect and change tf-devops/dev; it must not
   drift into tf-azure-hub because homepage/ACA-related controls were seen in an
   earlier Azure request.

3. AWS TOPOLOGY.
   AWS non-prod is under `tf-devops/terraform/dev_aws`:
   `dev`, `minidev`, `bolt`, `bolt_dr`, `bolt_sqlstaging`, `dev_devops`,
   `dev_sqlstaging`, `global`, `minidev_sqlstaging`, `observe`.

   AWS prod is under `tf-devops/terraform/prod_aws`:
   `ca3`, `ca3_dr`, `devops`, `eu1`, `eu1_dr`, `eu2`, `eu2_dr`, `global`,
   `observe`, `sqlstaging`, `sqlstaging_ca`, `sqlstaging_eu`, `sqlstaging_eu2`,
   `sqlstaging_us4`, `sqlstaging_west`, `us1`, `us1_dr`, `us2`, `us2_dr`,
   `us3`, `us3_dr`, `us4`, `us4_dr`. `root/global` maps to
   `tf-devops/terraform/root/global`.

4. AZURE TOPOLOGY.
   Azure non-prod is in tf-azure-hub:
   - `npr-int` -> `vars/npr/npr-int`
   - `npr-stg` / `npr-staging` -> `vars/npr/npr-stg`
   - `sbx-infra` / `sandbox` -> `vars/sbx/sbx-infra`

   Azure prod is in tf-azure-hub:
   - `ca4` or `prd-ca4` -> `vars/prd/prd-ca4`
   - `eu3` or `prd-eu3` -> `vars/prd/prd-eu3`
   - `us5` or `prd-us5` -> `vars/prd/prd-us5`
   - `us6` or `prd-us6` -> `vars/prd/prd-us6`

5. DEFAULT TARGETS AFTER CLOUD IS KNOWN.
   If the user requests an infrastructure change without naming an environment,
   do not ask for one merely because several environments exist. Use:
   - AWS default non-prod target: `minidev`
   - Azure default non-prod target: `sbx-infra`
   These defaults choose an environment, never a cloud. The cloud must already
   be resolved from the prompt/backend workflow.

6. MULTI-ENVIRONMENT REQUESTS ARE SETS.
   If the user names two or more environments, perform the requested change in
   every resolved target. If the user says `all`/`every` prod environment or
   `all`/`every` non-prod environment for a cloud, apply the change to every
   target supplied by `backend_environment_resolution.targets`.

   Never reduce a multi-target request to the first environment. Before return,
   verify that `files[]` covers every resolved target. Shared files may appear
   once only when live repository evidence proves they are shared.

7. NO CROSS-REPOSITORY MEMORY BLEED.
   A previous Azure flag/module mapping does not make the next `dev`, `minidev`,
   `us1`, etc. request Azure. A previous AWS module does not make an `npr-int`,
   `sandbox`, `ca4`, etc. request AWS. Conversation memory may retain the user
   request and prior choices, but current environment resolution + live GitHub
   evidence always selects the repository for the current turn.

8. CLARIFICATION POLICY.
   Do not ask the user to confirm cloud, repository, environment, folder, or
   tfvars path when backend_environment_resolution has resolved them. Ask only
   when its `error` is non-empty, when the user explicitly provided conflicting
   cloud/environment identifiers, or when more than one prompt-relevant
   resource/flag/module remains after live repository semantic filtering.

9. TARGET FILE SELECTION AFTER ENVIRONMENT RESOLUTION.
   First enter only the resolved repository/environment scope. Then inspect live
   repository files to find the prompt-related resource/module/flag. For Azure
   feature flags, prefer the resolved environment's `hub.tfvars` unless the
   user explicitly requested DR/failover, then inspect `dr.tfvars`. Do not jump
   from `hub.tfvars` to `dr.tfvars` because an earlier turn used DR.

10. SELF-VALIDATION BEFORE GENERATION/REPAIR RETURN.
    Confirm all of the following before returning files:
    - cloud == backend_environment_resolution.cloud;
    - repo_target == backend_environment_resolution.repo_target;
    - every changed path belongs to one of the resolved target environments or
      is a repository-proven shared companion file;
    - every requested target environment is represented in the generated write
      set;
    - no file from another cloud/repository or unrelated environment was added;
    - repair attempts preserve the same resolved target set and do not ask the
      user to reconfirm environment placement.
